# Opportunistan!
Tamil title: சந்தர்ப்பஸ்தான்!

For three hours, a life-and-death struggle took place.

Battle! Battle! A terrible battle!

They struck, delivered blows, hit faces, tore out entrails, broke waists, smashed heads, crushed people to pieces, wiped away insults, drank life, settled vengeance and went on their way!

In just three hours, enough battle occurred to require description in the ornate Tamil of *Manonmaniam*.

The battle that began at one in the afternoon on May 23 continued without stopping until four in the evening.

“Who fought whom?” you will ask.

The battle occurred between Pakistan and those belonging to Pakhtunistan.

“Pakhtunistan? Where is it?” Though still in the womb, it has begun to reveal its form and splendour and has even come forward to wage battle.

When Kabul Radio cried out about this battle, thoughtful people must have sharpened their ears and listened.

Pakhtunistan is still a bud, yet it spreads fragrance, and the scent it gives off is revolutionary.

Pakhtunistan is a growing shoot. Even before the shoot has become a leaf, it has grown strong enough to fight such a battle.

Why should Pakistan and Pakhtunistan fight? Is there some enmity, hatred or hostility? No. Yet the Pakhtuns are attempting to live separately from Pakistan, though not to be torn apart from one another. That is why there is conflict.

We have previously described the birth of Pakhtunistan.

India's rulers support this separatist agitation. After speaking against partition and then losing Pakistan, they now provide space in India for propaganda supporting separation within Pakistan. This is the accusation made by the Pakistan government.

It is not an empty accusation; evidence is offered.

Speaking from the Delhi radio station, Afghan representative Sardar Najibullah Khan said that the condition of their Pakhtun brothers in Pakistan was wretched.

Pakistan has pointed this out.

It has said that providing space for such propaganda is wrong and contrary to international law.

Our concern is not whether it is legally wrong. If a separatist agitation occurs in Pakistan, these people support it. They do not hesitate to offer incense or even fan its flames.

Not merely radio: they turn every cannon of propaganda towards it.

Indian newspapers carry numerous reports about Pakhtunistan. When Pakistan requested that they be stopped, Prime Minister Nehru said he could not intervene.

The very dignitaries who declared that Pakistan must not exist now support Pakhtunistan.

They offer explanations and interpretations of every kind to suit that position.

They prescribe one justice for others and another for themselves.

When a song of separation is sung in Pakistan, they beat time to it. But when the same song is heard in India, they cover their ears, roll their eyes and issue threats.

Even if a thousand reasons like those advanced for Pakhtunistan are offered for separation in India, they say that such a disposition must not exist.

If they oppose separation, they must oppose it everywhere and always. People incapable even of maintaining that political obstinacy are the ones speaking of courage.

They claim not merely to oppose all partition, but also to walk the path of Mahatma Gandhi. Nonviolence is said to be both their weapon and political method.

The Pakhtunistan movement, however, warns that it will follow the path of violence. More than that, it sometimes conducts armed struggle.

These people have added great value to the maxim “One thing in speech and another in action.” Their tongues therefore rotate in every direction imaginable, and they speak whichever way their minds happen to move.

Read the speech delivered by Maulana Mohammad Akbar Khan, president of the Pakhtun conference held in Calcutta, and you will know the nature of their path.

The conference president says the great powers of the world must quickly find a just remedy for the Pakhtunistan question in order to prevent armed conflict between the Pakhtuns and Pakistan.

The Pakistan government says India supports this agitation.

Whatever hardships and losses may come, the Pakhtuns are not prepared to live under Pakistani rule.

The Pakistan government sows hatred and violence against Pakhtunistan.

The government attempts to deceive ordinary people in the name of religion.

Instead of holding an election in the frontier region, all Khudai Khidmatgars should be released and an election conducted upon the Pakhtunistan question itself.

Through their conference, the Pakhtuns asked India always to assist them in this matter.

They demand Pakhtunistan on such grounds. India supports them, and Pakistan calls that support contrary to international law.

But the Nagas living in Assam have begun asking for Nagastan.

Has the Indian government accepted this? No.

When a separatist agitation occurs in Pakistan, they encourage it. When a similar feeling appears in India, they deliver sermons.

We have already written about Nagastan. Do you know what they say there? They speak sweetly: “There must be no separatist mentality.”

See how contradictory are the points made by Assam Provincial Congress president Siddhinath Sarma in his appeal to the Naga people:

> They must abandon separatist tendencies and live united with the Indian people, considering their own prosperity and the prosperity of the country.

> If the hill communities point out any grievance, the authorities will take suitable steps to remedy it.

They preach in this manner when Nagastan is demanded. Why do they not say the same to those asking for Pakhtunistan?

They are not opponents of separation. They oppose it only when it affects them. That is the affliction produced by selfishness.

Otherwise, would they support one separation and oppose another?

We demand Dravida Nadu.

We have called history as witness, cited culture as a reason, demonstrated ethnic difference, and offered character, customs and practices as evidence.

For many years, we have beaten the drum of this separation.

At first they ignored the Dravida Nadu separatist agitation. Then they raised an outcry. Today, there is no one in Congress who does not condemn it.

> What use is a tongue
> that does not abuse Dravida Nadu?
> What use are ears
> that do not hear that abuse?

Those still clinging to Congress continue singing in this manner.

Even so, we continue our work without relenting.

Those who ignored us later mocked us. Those who mocked us later condemned us. Those who condemned us now yearn to stop us. It is this gradually growing movement that the “election parrots” now speak about recklessly throughout the country.

As the election approaches, their speeches expand.

They have begun indiscriminately disparaging our national question in order to fill their party's ballot boxes.

But we know who these people are.

We refer not only to how they behaved when Pakistan was achieved. We also remind readers of the method they follow today on the Pakhtunistan question.

How did they oppose Pakistan? What did they say? Everyone capable of speech abused it. Everyone who knew how to write denounced it. Yet in the end they consented to Pakistan.

“Mother India” did not die because Pakistan separated, nor did cow slaughter result from it.

Hindustan stands beside Pakistan. The two have concluded a trade agreement. Karachi and Delhi are not two military camps but the capitals of two great countries.

Those who opposed separation later advised the Pakistan movement where and how the division should be made.

The Pakistan movement accused them at first of refusing to accept separation. Today they support a separatist agitation arising within Pakistan. What a strange change in the political sky!

Will those who support Pakhtunistan fail one day to support Dravidastan?

We offer reasons more numerous and more compelling than those advanced for Nagastan and Pakhtunistan to show why our Dravida Nadu must stand separately. Yet though they possess ears, they become deaf; though they possess mouths, they become mute. Now, after becoming deaf, they have turned into loudmouths.

When they found that sermons could not suppress us, they unleashed “election drums” in the hope of shouting us down.

We are neither alarmed nor shaken by them.

Ambalavan next door has a daughter, and she is a widow.

The widowed daughter became a revolutionary woman. She began arguing with her father that she must remarry.

Paramasivam from the neighbouring house spoke in her support: “Ah, is that not just? Though she lost her `thali` at so young an age, her feelings and heart have not died.”

But Paramasivam had a daughter in his own home. She did not even ask for widow remarriage; she asked permission to marry a man of another caste whom she loved. She wept and pleaded. He stopped her.

The Indian government behaves like Paramasivam next door.

The neighbour's widow is Pakistan's Pakhtunistan. Like Paramasivam, the Indian government speaks in its support.

But just as he prevents his own daughter's love marriage, the government opposes Dravidastan within India.

Speaking to suit the occasion may perhaps not appear wrong in a private individual. In a government, such conduct is dangerous.

Just as there are Pakistan, Hindustan, Pakhtunistan and Dravidastan, these people roam within Opportunistan.

Opportunistan will always turn into Troublestan!

(*Dravida Nadu*, 10 June 1951)

---NOTES---
The coined title `சந்தர்ப்பஸ்தான்` combines “circumstance/opportunity” with the suffix `-stan`; “Opportunistan” preserves the satire. Its closing counterpart `சங்கடஸ்தான்` is rendered “Troublestan.” `பக்டூனிஸ்தான்` is Pakhtunistan or Pashtunistan, and `குதாய் கித்மத்கார்` denotes the Khudai Khidmatgar movement. Several proper names are OCR-damaged, including Sardar Najibullah Khan and Maulana Mohammad Akbar Khan; the English follows the recoverable printed forms and context without altering the Tamil source. The violent opening is the article's rhetorical description of clashes connected to the Pakhtunistan movement, not an endorsement.
